<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- A crop-loss claim is worthless without a photo of the damage. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA"/>

    <!-- A photo only counts as evidence if it was taken on the farmer's own plot,
         and the geofence needs GPS-grade fixes to tell adjacent plots apart.
         COARSE must be declared alongside FINE on Android 12+, where the user can
         downgrade to approximate location; without it the request fails outright
         instead of degrading. No background location: the fix is taken at the
         moment of capture, with the app open. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION"/>

    <!-- Reports queue offline and upload when signal returns; NETWORK_STATE is
         how the app notices it has, instead of burning battery on blind retries. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"/>

    <!-- A claim waits on this phone until its evidence is complete, and until
         then the office has never heard of it. If the farmer walks away
         mid-walk, a notification from this phone is the only thing that can
         say so. Nothing leaves the handset: no server is told and no message
         is sent, so it works in a village with no signal — which is where an
         unfinished claim is most likely to be sitting. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>

    <!-- A claim is about 18 MB of video, and on a village connection that is
         minutes. A farmer who pockets the phone mid-upload used to stop it: the
         send ran off a thirty-second timer the moment the screen slept, with
         WorkManager waking behind it roughly every fifteen minutes. The trial
         lost most of an hour that way.

         DATA_SYNC is the service type Android 14+ requires to be declared for
         exactly this: finishing a transfer the user started. It runs only while
         an upload is in flight and shows a notification the whole time, so
         nothing happens out of the farmer's sight. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC"/>

    <!-- RECORD_AUDIO is not declared here, and it is in the built APK, and as of
         the live guidance work it is genuinely used.

         It was not, for most of this app's life, and the note below is kept because
         the reasoning still governs the walk: the camera records with
         enableAudio: false and the walk never asks for the microphone. What changed
         is that a farmer may now hold a spoken conversation with the assistant, and
         that needs the microphone at runtime.

         It is requested at the point of consent and nowhere else. Not at launch, not
         in the access flow with the camera and the location: a farmer who has
         declined the assistant, or whose farm cannot carry it, is never asked for a
         microphone they will not use. See core/live_consent.dart and
         live/live_session.dart.

         The original note follows, because it is why the permission was left in the
         manifest rather than stripped, and that decision is unchanged.
         The camera plugin's Android implementation declares it unconditionally
         and manifest merging brings it in, so the farmer is asked for
         microphone access this app never uses: the camera runs with
         enableAudio: false and nothing else records.

         It could be stripped with tools:node="remove". It has not been,
         deliberately: with audio disabled CameraX does not need the
         permission, but "does not need" is a judgement about every Android
         build in Maharashtra, and the cost of being wrong is a farmer standing
         in a field whose phone will not record. That is a worse outcome than an
         unused permission. Worth removing once it can be tried on real
         handsets; see docs/FIELD_TRIAL_2026-09-28.md.

         One handset now says so: a Vivo V2253 on Android 15 recorded four
         station clips with the microphone permission never requested and never
         granted. That is evidence, not yet a decision, because it is one
         vendor's camera stack and the claim being tested is about all of them.
         Worth stripping after the same result on a second vendor, ideally a
         Samsung or a Xiaomi, since those are what the trial districts carry.

         BLUETOOTH, VIBRATE, MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS and
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SHORT_SERVICE arrive by manifest merge from
         flutter_webrtc, which is there for the spoken assistant.

         MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS is genuinely used: WebRTC routes audio between the
         earpiece and the speaker, and a conversation on a handheld phone needs
         that. The other three are not. BLUETOOTH is for headsets, which nobody in
         a field is wearing; VIBRATE and the short-service permission are for
         call-style notifications this app does not raise.

         They have not been stripped with tools:node="remove", for the reason
         RECORD_AUDIO was not: "does not need" is a judgement about every Android
         build in Maharashtra, and removing a permission a media stack expects is
         the kind of change that fails on one vendor's handset in a field. Worth
         removing once the assistant has actually been used on real phones, which
         it has not yet been.

         The size is the larger cost and is worth stating plainly: the native
         WebRTC stack takes the release APK from 22.3 MB to 34.2 MB, a 12 MB
         increase on every handset, for a feature that is off unless a build turns
         it on. If the trial does not need it, a build without flutter_webrtc is
         12 MB smaller and the assistant still works over the WebSocket, which is what
         the fallback is for.

         RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and WAKE_LOCK arrive by manifest merge from
         flutter_foreground_task and are not used by this app: autoRunOnBoot is
         false, so nothing here starts at boot. They are declared by the plugin
         rather than by us, and removing them would mean forking it. -->
    <application
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- The service that keeps an upload alive across a locked screen.
             The class name is the plugin's and must not be changed; the type is
             declared here rather than in the plugin because Android 14+ requires
             the app to state which foreground service types it uses, and
             dataSync is the only one this app has any business with. -->
        <service
            android:name="com.pravera.flutter_foreground_task.service.ForegroundService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
